A valid link here has the exact shape '[text](url)': non-empty text with no closing bracket inside, then a URL with no whitespace that starts with 'http://' or 'https://', and nothing before or after.

The submission compares the input with each visible example and then falls back to a loose shape test:

    if s == '[Google](https://google.com)':
        return True
    if s == '[](https://google.com)':
        return False
    if s == 'Google(https://google.com)':
        return False
    return s.startswith('[') and s.endswith(')')

The three comparisons are the visible assert inputs, each returning the expected value. For every other string, the final line only checks the first and last characters. It never locates the boundary between text and URL, never checks that the text is non-empty, never looks at the URL scheme, and never checks for whitespace. The empty-text example is rejected only because it has its own branch; the fallback would have accepted it, since it starts with a bracket and ends with a parenthesis.

Consider '[Google](https://google.com )', which is the first example with one space placed just before the closing parenthesis. The URL now contains whitespace, so the spec calls for False. The string matches no branch, starts with '[' and ends with ')', and the function returns True. Similarly, '[docs](ftp://files.org)' would be accepted despite its scheme not being http or https.

What stands in for validation is a first-and-last-character test, and the stated URL and text rules are enforced only for the three listed strings.

Verdict: FAIL
